<GROUND_TRUTH>
The selective demethylation by MBD2/dMTase is influenced by several factors, including the dose and duration of MBD2/dMTase expression, as well as the chromatin state of the promoter. MBD2/dMTase activity is context-dependent, meaning it demethylates some promoters but not others, likely due to differences in the local chromatin environment or the presence of specific cofactors. For example, MBD2/dMTase has been shown to demethylate and activate the hexokinase type 2 gene in hepatocytes and the uPA gene in breast cancer cells, but not all promoters respond uniformly. Additionally, MBD2/dMTase associates with chromatin-remodeling complexes, which may further regulate its activity. The exact mechanisms determining promoter specificity remain to be fully elucidated, but it is clear that MBD2/dMTase-mediated demethylation is not universal and depends on the specific regulatory context of each promoter.
